/-!
# The decomposition group of a finite place is cyclic modulo the level subgroup

Let `k` be a number field, `Ω` an algebraic closure of `k`, `B` a finite place of `Ω` (a
`ValuationSubring Ω` whose trace on `k` is not everything) and `M ⊆ Ω` a finite normal
intermediate field.  This file proves

`Work.SSGRoute.levelSub_commutator_and_generator`:

* the commutator of two elements of the decomposition group `D_B = SSGRoute.decomp k B` lies in the
  level subgroup `levelSub B M` (the elements of `D_B` acting trivially on `B ∩ M` modulo its
  maximal ideal);
* `D_B` is generated by a single element modulo `levelSub B M`.

Equivalently: `D_B ⧸ levelSub B M` is a finite cyclic group.

## The proof

Everything happens at the finite level `M`, using the classical theorem that the decomposition
group of a prime of a finite Galois extension of number fields is generated by a Frobenius element
modulo its inertia subgroup:

* `P = levelPrime k Ω B hB M` is the prime of `𝓞 M` cut out by `B`
  (`B.comap (algebraMap ↥M Ω) = 𝒪_{M,P}`, `NK.Reconstruct.primeOfLevel_spec`);
* `Work.LocalFields.exists_generator_mod_inertia` provides `f ∈ D_P` which generates `D_P` modulo
  the inertia subgroup `I_P = Ideal.inertia Gal(M/k) P.asIdeal` (the exact sequence
  `D_P ⧸ I_P ≃* Gal(κ_P/κ_𝔭)` of ramification theory together with the cyclicity of the Galois
  group of a finite extension of finite fields); in particular `D_P ⧸ I_P` is cyclic;
* the restriction `ρ : D_B →* Gal(M/k)` maps `D_B` into `D_P`, and
  `Work.SSGRoute.valuation_sub_lt_one_of_isTrivialAtLevel` shows that an element whose restriction
  to `M` is trivial modulo `P` lies in `levelSub B M`.

The two conclusions are then obtained from the single fact that the image of `D_B` in the cyclic
group `D_P ⧸ I_P` is itself cyclic: it is abelian (giving the commutators) and generated by the
image of one element `g ∈ D_B` (giving the generator).
-/

open scoped Pointwise NumberField

